.TH TUI_SCREEN_NEW 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_screen_new, tui_screen_free, tui_screen_resize, tui_screen_rows,
tui_screen_cols, tui_screen_clear, tui_screen_put, tui_screen_fill,
tui_screen_set_cursor, tui_screen_invalidate, tui_screen_set_sync,
tui_screen_render \- a grid of styled cells and its diffing renderer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/screen.h>
.PP
.B typedef struct tui_style { tui_color fg, bg; uint16_t attrs; } tui_style;
.B TUI_STYLE_DEFAULT
.BR TUI_STYLE_HEADER ", " TUI_STYLE_SELECTION ", " TUI_STYLE_ACCENT ", " TUI_STYLE_OK ,
.BR TUI_STYLE_ERROR ", " TUI_STYLE_HINT ", " TUI_STYLE_PROGRESS
.BI "tui_style tui_style_make(tui_color " fg ", tui_color " bg ", uint16_t " attrs );
.BI "tui_style tui_style_attrs(uint16_t " attrs );
.BI "int       tui_style_eq(tui_style " a ", tui_style " b );
.PP
.BI "tui_screen *tui_screen_new(int " rows ", int " cols );
.BI "void tui_screen_free(tui_screen *" s );
.BI "int  tui_screen_resize(tui_screen *" s ", int " rows ", int " cols );
.BI "int  tui_screen_rows(const tui_screen *" s );
.BI "int  tui_screen_cols(const tui_screen *" s );
.BI "void tui_screen_clear(tui_screen *" s ", tui_style " style );
.BI "int  tui_screen_put(tui_screen *" s ", int " row ", int " col ", int " max_cols ,
.BI "                    const char *" utf8 ", tui_style " style );
.BI "void tui_screen_fill(tui_screen *" s ", int " row ", int " col ", int " n ,
.BI "                     uint32_t " cp ", tui_style " style );
.BI "void tui_screen_set_cursor(tui_screen *" s ", int " row ", int " col ", int " visible );
.BI "void tui_screen_invalidate(tui_screen *" s );
.BI "void tui_screen_set_sync(tui_screen *" s ", int " on );
.BI "long tui_screen_render(tui_screen *" s ", tui_buf *" out );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A screen holds two grids of cells: the back grid, which drawing changes,
and the front grid, what the terminal is known to show.
.BR tui_screen_render ()
appends to
.I out
the bytes that bring the terminal from the front grid to the back grid,
and then records the back grid as shown.
The screen writes to no descriptor: the program writes the buffer (see
.BR tui_term_flush_buf (3)),
and a test can feed the same bytes to a terminal emulator.
.SS Styles
A colour is
.BR TUI_COLOR_DEFAULT ,
one of 16
.RB ( TUI_COLOR_16 (n),
.BR TUI_RED ,
.BR TUI_BRIGHT_BLUE ,
\&...), one of 256
.RB ( TUI_COLOR_256 (n)),
or direct
.RB ( TUI_COLOR_RGB (r,g,b)).
The attributes are
.BR TUI_ATTR_BOLD ,
.BR TUI_ATTR_DIM ,
.BR TUI_ATTR_ITALIC ,
.B TUI_ATTR_UNDERLINE
.BR TUI_ATTR_REVERSE ,
.B TUI_ATTR_STRIKE
and
.BR TUI_ATTR_BLINK .
.PP
The workspace palette gives each role of the interface one style, and
the widgets use them as their defaults:
.TS
l l.
\fBTUI_STYLE_HEADER\fP	bold white on blue: the header bar
\fBTUI_STYLE_SELECTION\fP	bold reverse video: what the cursor is on
\fBTUI_STYLE_ACCENT\fP	bold cyan: a modal's frame, a focused label
\fBTUI_STYLE_OK\fP	bold green: a write that worked
\fBTUI_STYLE_ERROR\fP	bold red: a refusal, a failure
\fBTUI_STYLE_HINT\fP	yellow: key hints, secondary columns
\fBTUI_STYLE_PROGRESS\fP	italic yellow: what is being waited for
.TE
.PP
All but the hints carry an attribute besides their colours, so that a
selection, a frame or an error is told apart without colour; hints are
told apart by where they stand.
.SS Drawing
.BR tui_screen_put ()
writes UTF-8 text from (\fIrow\fR, \fIcol\fR), at most
.I max_cols
columns (0 or less: to the right edge), clipped to the screen, and
returns the columns written.
Every character is passed through
.BR tui_cp_sanitize (3),
so a control or an escape sequence in the text is drawn as U+00B7 and
never reaches the terminal.
The bidirectional controls (U+061C, U+200E, U+200F, U+202A..U+202E and
U+2066..U+2069) are dropped and take no column, so text from an untrusted
source cannot make a terminal reorder what is around it.
A wide character takes two cells and is never drawn by halves; writing
over half of one blanks its other half.
A combining mark joins the character before it in the same text (a cell
keeps up to two marks; more are not drawn); a mark with no character
before it is not drawn.
.PP
.BR tui_screen_fill ()
fills
.I n
cells with
.I cp
(a space if
.I cp
is not one column wide);
.BR tui_screen_clear ()
fills the whole screen with blanks in a style.
.PP
.BR tui_screen_set_cursor ()
says where the terminal cursor is left after a render, and whether it is
shown; it is hidden by default.
.SS Rendering
The output uses only sequences xterm-compatible terminals have had for
decades: CUP to position, SGR for styles (always starting from a reset, so
no attribute leaks from one cell into the next), ED 2 to clear, DECTCEM
for the cursor.
Every changed cell is written; no relative movement, no erase-to-end of
line, no scrolling, so the result does not depend on how a terminal treats
the cells around it.
A full frame \(em the first one, and the next one after
.BR tui_screen_invalidate ()
or
.BR tui_screen_resize ()
\(em clears the screen and writes only the cells that are not default
blanks.
Each frame with any output is wrapped in synchronized-update mode
.RB ( "CSI ? 2026 h" " .. " "CSI ? 2026 l" ),
which terminals that do not know it ignore;
.BR tui_screen_set_sync ()
turns it off.
.PP
Call
.BR tui_screen_invalidate ()
when something else wrote to the terminal (a log line, a child process):
the screen no longer knows what the terminal shows.
Call
.BR tui_screen_resize ()
on every
.BR TUI_EV_RESIZE ,
even with an unchanged size: the content is cleared and the next render
draws everything.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR tui_screen_new ()
returns the screen, or NULL with
.I errno
.B EINVAL
(a size of 0 or less, or too large) or
.BR ENOMEM .
.BR tui_screen_resize ()
returns 0, or \-1 with
.I errno
and the screen unchanged.
.BR tui_screen_render ()
returns the number of bytes appended (0 when nothing changed), or \-1 with
.I errno
.B ENOMEM
when the buffer failed; the frame is then not recorded as shown and the
next render is a full one.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_canvas_of (3),
.BR tui_buf_init (3),
.BR tui_cp_width (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
